### Vulnerability Analysis

In `app/assets/javascripts/discourse/app/lib/mobile.js`, the `Mobile.reloadPage` function directly passes `window.location.pathname` concatenated with a query string into `window.location.assign`:

```javascript
  reloadPage(mobile) {
    window.location.assign(
      window.location.pathname + "?mobile_view=" + (mobile ? "1" : "0")
    );
  },
```

When an attacker crafts a URL where the path begins with a double slash (e.g., `https://forum.example.com//evil.com?mobile_view=1`), the browser sets `window.location.pathname` to `"//evil.com"`.

On initialization of Discourse's client application (`Mobile.init()`, invoked on application startup via `instance-initializers/mobile.js`), the query parameter `mobile_view=1` or `mobile_view=0` is inspected:
```javascript
    if (window.location.search.match(/mobile_view=1/)) {
      localStorage.mobileView = true;
    }
    ...
    if (localStorage.mobileView) {
      let savedValue = localStorage.mobileView === "true";
      if (savedValue !== this.mobileView) {
        this.reloadPage(savedValue);
      }
    }
```
If a desktop client visits `https://forum.example.com//evil.com?mobile_view=1`, `savedValue` becomes `true` while `this.mobileView` is `false`. This triggers `this.reloadPage(true)`.

Inside `reloadPage(mobile)`, `window.location.assign` receives `"//evil.com?mobile_view=1"`. Because standard browser URL resolution interprets strings beginning with `//` as protocol-relative URLs (RFC 3986 network-path references), the browser performs an open redirection to `https://evil.com/?mobile_view=1`.

### Data Flow
- **Source**: Attacker-controlled URL in `window.location.pathname` and `window.location.search`.
- **Validation**: None. `window.location.pathname` is neither sanitized nor checked against leading double slashes (`//`).
- **Sink**: `window.location.assign(...)` in `reloadPage` at line 54.

### Conceptual Exploit Payload
A victim clicking the link:
`https://forum.example.com//attacker.com?mobile_view=1`
will load the Discourse application shell, trigger `Mobile.init()`, and immediately be redirected to `https://attacker.com/?mobile_view=1`.
